# Test: a chain argument holding a backslash is authorized with the backslash intact
#
# The one question: do the authorizer and the accountant read their tokens from
# the partition's chain-grammar scan, so a chain argument holding a backslash
# outside quotes (`| match a\b`) reaches the TACACS+ server as typed and an
# allowed line runs? The invocation scan of the command part refuses a
# backslash (ErrBackslashInInvocation); tokenizing the chain through it would
# deny the line or record it as one token. Only a daemon proves the SSH route,
# isAuthorized and the TACACS+ client agree on the token.
#
# VALIDATES: spec-pipe-chain-honored-on-every-command-surface AC-38
#   (authorization half), and the AC-6 survivor: a backslash outside quotes in
#   the chain is a literal character.
# PREVENTS: the chain read by a second, stricter scan for AAA than for the
#   handler, so a line the handler accepts is refused or mis-recorded.
#
# Red today: SSH dispatches the command part alone, so the AUTHOR request is
#   `system command list` and holds no `match a\b`.
#
# Accounting half of AC-38: the START record holds `match a\b` as one
# cmd-arg, with the backslash intact. `le mock tacacs` prints the command an
# ACCT record carries (internal/test/mock/tacacs parseAcctCommand), quoted
# the same way as the AUTHOR line (see Method).
#
# Method: pipe-chain-unauthorized-refused.ci's daemon and mock, with no deny
# rule. One typed backslash prints as FOUR in the mock's line, and each
# doubling is a contract: the TACACS+ client sends a cmd-arg holding a
# backslash in its reversible printable-ASCII form (asciiCommandValue,
# internal/component/tacacs/command_text.go: `a\b` goes on the wire as
# `a\\b`), and the mock quotes what it received (records.go Record.Line,
# strconv.Quote), so the line holds `a\\\\b`; the patterns match those four
# characters. Two would be the client sending the token unescaped, and eight
# a second escape on the daemon's side. The quotes print plain: the
# standalone mock writes its own stderr.

option=timeout:value=60s
option=env:var=ZE_CONFIG_DIR:value=.
option=env:var=XDG_DATA_HOME:value=.
option=env:var=ze.ssh.password:value=testpass

stdin=init-answers:terminator=EOF_INIT
admin
testpass
127.0.0.1
$PORT2
EOF_INIT

tmpfs=aaa.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
system {
	authentication {
		tacacs {
			server 127.0.0.1 { port $PORT; key "ze-mock-key"; }
			timeout 2
			authorization true
			accounting true
		}
		tacacs-profile 15 { profile [ admin ]; }
	}
	authorization {
		profile admin { run { default-action allow; } edit { default-action allow; } }
	}
}
environment {
	ssh {
		enabled true
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1;
			port $PORT2;
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=le mock tacacs --port $PORT --key ze-mock-key --user admin:testpass:15:name=tacacs:ready=le mock tacacs: listening on
expect=stderr:pattern=AUTHOR cmd="system command list \| match a\\\\\\\\b \| count \| json" reply=PASS_ADD
expect=stderr:pattern=ACCT START seq=[0-9]+ total=[0-9]+ cmd="system command list \| match a\\\\\\\\b \| count \| json"
reject=stderr:contains=reply=FAIL

cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze init:stdin=init-answers:exit=0
cmd=background:seq=3:exec=ze start aaa.conf:name=daemon:ready=Ze running.

# Allowed and run: `match` receives a, a literal backslash and b; no command
# name holds them, so the generic count answers 0.
cmd=foreground:seq=4:exec=ze cli -c "system command list | match a\b | count | json":exit=0
expect=stdout:pattern="count": ?0
reject=stderr:contains=backslash

cmd=stop:seq=5:name=daemon:signal=term
cmd=stop:seq=6:name=tacacs:signal=term
